🛠️ Strategy Overview
We solve this in two clean phases:

Phase 1: Find All Deepest Nodes (BFS)
Why BFS?
Breadth-First Search naturally traverses the tree level by level.
The last level visited in BFS contains all deepest nodes.
What we track during BFS:
A queue to traverse level by level.
A parent map to remember each node’s parent (needed later).
A list that always stores the current level’s nodes.
After BFS finishes, this list will contain only deepest nodes.
Result of Phase 1:
A list of all deepest nodes.
A mapping from every node → its parent.

Phase 2: Move Up Until All Nodes Meet (LCA Logic)
Now we have multiple deepest nodes. What next?

Idea:-
Put all deepest nodes into a set.
While the set contains more than one node:
Replace every node in the set with its parent.
Eventually, all paths will merge into a single node.
Why does this work?
All nodes move upward one level at a time.
The first point where all paths intersect is their Lowest Common Ancestor.
That node is the smallest subtree root that contains all deepest nodes.

✅ Final Outcome
If there is only one deepest node → return it.
If there are many → return their LCA, found by upward merging.

🧮 Complexity Analysis:-

⏱ Time Complexity: O(N:-
BFS Traversal: Visits each node once → O(N)
Upward merging:
In the worst case (a skewed tree), nodes move up O(H) times
Since H ≤ N, total time is still linear
➡️ Overall: O(N)

📦 Space Complexity: O(N)
Parent Map: Stores parent for every node → O(N)
Queue (BFS): Can store up to one full level → O(N)
Set of deepest nodes: At most one level → O(N)
➡️ Overall: O(N)

Code:-
### class Solution {
    public TreeNode subtreeWithAllDeepest(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return null;

        Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent = new HashMap<>();
        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);
        parent.put(root, null);

        List<TreeNode> lastLevel = new ArrayList<>();

        // BFS traversal
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int size = q.size();
            lastLevel.clear();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode node = q.poll();
                lastLevel.add(node);

                if (node.left != null) {
                    parent.put(node.left, node);
                    q.offer(node.left);
                }
                if (node.right != null) {
                    parent.put(node.right, node);
                    q.offer(node.right);
                }
            }
        }

        Set<TreeNode> deepest = new HashSet<>(lastLevel);

        // Move upward until all nodes converge
        while (deepest.size() > 1) {
            Set<TreeNode> next = new HashSet<>();
            for (TreeNode node : deepest) {
                next.add(parent.get(node));
            }
            deepest = next;
        }

        return deepest.iterator().next();
    }
}
